WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:02.940
I’m going to show you
two very short adverts.

00:00:02.940 --> 00:00:05.520
My question is:
which is louder?

00:00:06.300 --> 00:00:08.640
This video is sponsored by NordVPN.

00:00:09.104 --> 00:00:12.284
THIS VIDEO IS SPONSORED BY NORDVPN!

00:00:12.780 --> 00:00:14.940
Here’s the volume of the
nice, calm one.

00:00:14.940 --> 00:00:16.620
The second one?
With the shouting,

00:00:16.620 --> 00:00:17.760
the background noise,
the music?

00:00:17.760 --> 00:00:21.900
If you’re measuring volume
purely by decibels,

00:00:21.900 --> 00:00:23.940
then it was lower.

00:00:23.940 --> 00:00:25.440
But it definitely
sounded louder.

00:00:25.440 --> 00:00:26.520
To explain that,

00:00:26.520 --> 00:00:27.780
we need to start
at the beginning:

00:00:27.780 --> 00:00:30.840
what does it mean for
something to be loud?

00:00:31.800 --> 00:00:34.080
Let’s say a tree falls
in the forest.

00:00:34.080 --> 00:00:35.640
The tree causes
the air to move,

00:00:35.640 --> 00:00:37.080
both by pushing
it out the way,

00:00:37.080 --> 00:00:39.420
but also by the tree
and the things around it

00:00:39.420 --> 00:00:42.060
vibrating as they deal with
all the forces involved.

00:00:42.060 --> 00:00:44.700
Those vibrations are
transferred into the air.

00:00:44.700 --> 00:00:46.140
The actual air itself

00:00:46.140 --> 00:00:48.300
is not travelling from the
crashing tree to your ears,

00:00:48.300 --> 00:00:49.920
but rather it’s a wave

00:00:49.920 --> 00:00:52.380
caused by air molecules
bumping into each other.

00:00:52.380 --> 00:00:54.360
And if you’re standing nearby,

00:00:54.360 --> 00:00:56.280
then bits of that wave
get funnelled

00:00:56.280 --> 00:00:59.220
by the flappy bit of your ear,
the pinna, into your ear canal.

00:00:59.220 --> 00:01:02.640
The waves get to your eardrum,
and it vibrates in turn,

00:01:02.640 --> 00:01:04.560
just a tiny, tiny amount.

00:01:04.560 --> 00:01:07.440
Behind it, there’s the three
smallest bones in the human body:

00:01:07.440 --> 00:01:09.240
the hammer, anvil, and stirrup.

00:01:09.240 --> 00:01:11.700
They amplify those vibrations
through leverage,

00:01:11.700 --> 00:01:14.220
and deliver these vibrations
into the cochlea,

00:01:14.220 --> 00:01:15.660
shaped like a snail shell,

00:01:15.660 --> 00:01:17.880
which converts it into
electrical impulses

00:01:17.880 --> 00:01:20.520
that get interpreted by the brain.

00:01:20.520 --> 00:01:23.940
If you ask a physicist,
those air movements are sound.

00:01:23.940 --> 00:01:26.220
Ask a psychologist,
and they’ll say that

00:01:26.220 --> 00:01:28.860
until those movements get
interpreted by the brain,

00:01:28.860 --> 00:01:32.100
it’s just stuff moving,
because sound is in your head,

00:01:32.100 --> 00:01:33.120
it’s an experience.

00:01:33.120 --> 00:01:35.820
If the tree falls in the forest
and no-one’s there to listen,

00:01:35.820 --> 00:01:39.780
a physicist says there’s sound,
a psychologist says there isn’t.

00:01:39.780 --> 00:01:41.700
And both of them are correct

00:01:41.700 --> 00:01:44.400
because they’re using the same
word to mean different things,

00:01:44.400 --> 00:01:47.940
which is part of why it takes
so long to answer

00:01:47.940 --> 00:01:49.440
what seems like a simple question.

00:01:49.440 --> 00:01:51.660
So. Let’s talk about that wave.

00:01:51.660 --> 00:01:53.640
It’s got two important values:

00:01:53.640 --> 00:01:56.040
the amount of pressure for each peak,
the amplitude;

00:01:56.040 --> 00:01:58.980
and the time between each
peak arriving, the frequency.

00:01:58.980 --> 00:02:01.080
We’ll start with the amplitude.

00:02:01.080 --> 00:02:05.580
Pressure is measured in pascals,
and regular air at about sea level

00:02:05.580 --> 00:02:09.240
has a pressure of somewhere around
100,000 pascals:

00:02:09.240 --> 00:02:12.600
that’s the weight of all the
atmosphere above pushing down.

00:02:12.600 --> 00:02:15.960
Our ears can detect a fluctuation
of about 20 micropascals.

00:02:15.960 --> 00:02:18.360
A whisper in your ear would be
about 2 millipascals,

00:02:18.360 --> 00:02:20.520
and traffic noise
about 200 millipascals.

00:02:20.520 --> 00:02:22.560
And someone screaming at the
top of their voice,

00:02:22.560 --> 00:02:25.800
directly into your ear,
is around 20 pascals,

00:02:25.800 --> 00:02:28.680
which is well into the sorts of
pressure that can cause hearing loss.

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:34.020
Okay, as you might have noticed,
that’s not a particularly useful graph,

00:02:34.020 --> 00:02:36.180
because the differences
in scale are enormous,

00:02:36.180 --> 00:02:39.360
even if we get rid of the
ambient pressure at the top.

00:02:39.360 --> 00:02:42.060
So instead, let’s use a
logarithmic scale,

00:02:42.060 --> 00:02:44.220
which means that each block
going vertically

00:02:44.220 --> 00:02:46.140
is ten times the previous one.

00:02:46.140 --> 00:02:48.060
That’s much clearer:
just remember,

00:02:48.060 --> 00:02:50.100
this is a 10 times
jump in pressure,

00:02:50.100 --> 00:02:51.900
and so’s this,
and so’s this.

00:02:51.900 --> 00:02:53.880
That’s how decibels work.

00:02:53.880 --> 00:02:57.060
That minimum that humans can
detect, 20 micropascals,

00:02:57.060 --> 00:02:59.160
is taken as 0 bels,

00:02:59.160 --> 00:03:02.280
and then each 10-times increase
in pressure adds two bels,

00:03:02.280 --> 00:03:03.900
and we generally use
tenths of those,

00:03:03.900 --> 00:03:07.140
so each 10-times increase
is another 20 decibels.

00:03:07.140 --> 00:03:10.800
And I know, a load of sound engineers
and techies are already in the comments

00:03:10.800 --> 00:03:14.940
going “no, no, each ten times increase
is 10 decibels, you fool!”

00:03:14.940 --> 00:03:18.060
and here’s reason two why this
is so difficult to answer:

00:03:18.060 --> 00:03:20.280
there are multiple decibel scales!

00:03:20.280 --> 00:03:22.620
Because sound engineers
talk about intensity,

00:03:22.620 --> 00:03:24.000
the amount of power transmitted,

00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:26.640
not the amount of actual pressure
on your eardrum,

00:03:26.640 --> 00:03:28.440
those two things are proportional,

00:03:28.440 --> 00:03:31.260
and look, it doesn’t matter for this,

00:03:31.260 --> 00:03:34.980
just …blame mathematicians, okay?
The important thing is:

00:03:34.980 --> 00:03:39.360
your brain doesn’t interpret
“ten times more change in pressure”

00:03:39.360 --> 00:03:41.160
to mean “ten times as loud”.

00:03:41.160 --> 00:03:44.340
Sure, traffic noise feels
louder than a whisper,

00:03:44.340 --> 00:03:46.620
but I don’t think many people
would guess that the amplitude

00:03:46.620 --> 00:03:48.720
is a hundred times more pressure.

00:03:48.720 --> 00:03:50.040
That’s why we use decibels,

00:03:50.040 --> 00:03:53.460
a logarithmic scale matches
how we think about sound.

00:03:54.240 --> 00:03:55.140
Hold that thought.

00:03:55.140 --> 00:03:57.900
That’s amplitude dealt with.
Next is frequency.

00:03:57.900 --> 00:03:58.920
And that’s easy to define:

00:03:58.920 --> 00:04:01.680
it’s just the number of peaks per second,
or ‘hertz’.

00:04:01.680 --> 00:04:05.940
The human ear can detect roughly
from 20 Hz through to 20,000 Hz,

00:04:05.940 --> 00:04:08.460
although that starts to decrease
as people get older.

00:04:08.460 --> 00:04:11.220
The sound wave is going to be
much more complex, of course,

00:04:11.220 --> 00:04:13.320
it’s usually not transmitting
just a single frequency,

00:04:13.320 --> 00:04:16.440
but let’s not get into the
maths of that too much!

00:04:17.040 --> 00:04:22.260
We’re particularly sensitive to the
range between 2,000 and 5,000 Hz,

00:04:22.260 --> 00:04:25.620
which means our brains perceive
those frequencies as being louder.

00:04:25.620 --> 00:04:30.000
In 1933, Harvey Fletcher and
Wilden A. Munson published this,

00:04:30.540 --> 00:04:33.780
which became known as the
Fletcher-Munson curve.

00:04:33.780 --> 00:04:36.180
They played different sounds
at different levels

00:04:36.180 --> 00:04:39.240
and noted how their test subjects
perceived that sound.

00:04:39.240 --> 00:04:42.120
The curve has been updated and
refined over time,

00:04:42.120 --> 00:04:45.540
and now it’s usually called
the Equal Loudness Contour.

00:04:46.140 --> 00:04:47.940
So if you want this tone...

00:04:48.780 --> 00:04:49.740
and this tone...

00:04:50.400 --> 00:04:52.320
to feel as loud as each other,

00:04:52.320 --> 00:04:53.880
you need to play the low one…

00:04:53.880 --> 00:04:56.340
I want to say louder,
but that’s not right.

00:04:56.340 --> 00:04:59.040
You’d need to play the low one
with greater amplitude.

00:04:59.040 --> 00:05:02.400
And of course, science has a
measuring system for this too.

00:05:02.400 --> 00:05:05.940
Audio engineers and broadcast companies
came up with LUFS,

00:05:05.940 --> 00:05:08.280
Loudness Units Full Scale.

00:05:08.280 --> 00:05:11.640
The calculations for this take
the loudness contour into account,

00:05:11.640 --> 00:05:14.040
along with how long
the loud sounds last.

00:05:14.040 --> 00:05:18.000
LUFS are a scientific way to measure
how loud something should feel,

00:05:18.000 --> 00:05:20.760
not how much the air
happens to be moving.

00:05:20.760 --> 00:05:23.580
If you’re making a video
for YouTube or Netflix,

00:05:23.580 --> 00:05:25.560
or music for Spotify or iTunes,

00:05:25.560 --> 00:05:27.180
there are guides for you
to follow.

00:05:27.180 --> 00:05:30.360
YouTube asks you to
aim for -14 LUFS

00:05:30.360 --> 00:05:33.420
– the units are kinda arbitrary,
it’s just a number.

00:05:33.420 --> 00:05:35.880
If you upload something that’s
louder than that,

00:05:35.880 --> 00:05:39.180
they’ll just turn the player’s
volume setting down automatically,

00:05:39.180 --> 00:05:41.640
in the background, so people
don’t suddenly get startled

00:05:41.640 --> 00:05:44.400
if one video or music track
is louder than another.

00:05:44.400 --> 00:05:48.420
So that means if you’re making
a movie or a TV show,

00:05:48.420 --> 00:05:50.280
and you want your loud sounds
to have impact,

00:05:51.060 --> 00:05:53.100
you can’t turn them up
above the limit,

00:05:53.100 --> 00:05:56.400
you have to turn the dialog and speech
and all the other sounds down.

00:05:56.400 --> 00:05:59.160
Not too much, that’ll also
cause problems, but enough

00:05:59.160 --> 00:06:03.480
that when the big explosion happens,
it’s noticeable. It's audible.

00:06:04.020 --> 00:06:06.480
But advertisers don’t need
or want the nuance,

00:06:06.480 --> 00:06:07.500
they just want to be heard,

00:06:07.500 --> 00:06:10.320
they usually want to be as loud
as they can be throughout.

00:06:10.920 --> 00:06:15.060
The European Union specifies -23 LUFS
as its target for broadcasters,

00:06:15.060 --> 00:06:17.880
to try and keep adverts and
programmes at the same loudness.

00:06:17.880 --> 00:06:21.180
The US has no specific guidelines
but says that broadcasters

00:06:21.180 --> 00:06:22.830
should try to match the two –

00:06:22.830 --> 00:06:25.860
although that rule is
rarely, if ever, enforced.

00:06:25.860 --> 00:06:30.240
All of which is to say that the whisper
of that first advert?

00:06:30.240 --> 00:06:32.520
The sound waves had higher amplitude,

00:06:32.520 --> 00:06:35.340
your eardrum will have vibrated
further back and forth.

00:06:35.340 --> 00:06:37.440
But it didn’t feel as loud.

00:06:37.440 --> 00:06:40.200
There were more decibels,
but fewer LUFS.

00:06:40.200 --> 00:06:43.537
And these days,
it’s the LUFS that matter.

